.TH KERNEL_COMMAND_LINE 7 "August 2026" "Substrate" "Miscellaneous Information Manual"
.SH NAME
kernel_command_line \- Substrate kernel command-line parameters
.SH DESCRIPTION
Substrate accepts a whitespace-separated kernel command line from the bootloader.
Parameters are either boolean flags such as
.B serial_debug
or key/value pairs such as
.BR console=serial0 .
.PP
The
.B debug=
parameter enables named kernel debug channels without forcing every verbose
path on globally.
.SH SYNTAX
.BI debug= channel [ ,channel ... ]
.PP
Examples:
.IP \(bu 2
.B debug=syscall
.IP \(bu
.B debug=elf,perso:linux
.IP \(bu
.B debug=trap,perso:elks:aout
.IP \(bu
.B debug=storage:ide
.SH CHANNEL MATCHING
Channel names are colon-delimited.
An exact match enables that channel.
A parent channel enables all descendants.
.PP
Examples:
.IP \(bu 2
.B debug=perso:elks
matches
.B perso:elks
and
.B perso:elks:aout
.IP \(bu
.B debug=perso
matches any
.B perso:*
channel
.IP \(bu
.B debug=all
or
.B debug=*
enables every channel
.SH CURRENT CHANNELS
The current in-tree channels are:
.TP
.B syscall
Enable syscall entry/return tracing.
This is the structured replacement for the legacy
.B syscall_trace
and
.B syscall_log
boot flags.
.TP
.B elf
Enable verbose ELF loader and exec handoff tracing.
.TP
.B trap
Log user trap-to-signal translations before normal signal delivery.
.TP
.B proc
Log process-tree transitions and
.BR waitpid (2)
search decisions, including child-linking, exit, and reap flow.
.TP
.B storage:ide
Enable IDE transport diagnostics, including DMA setup and completion tracing.
.TP
.B vm:brk
Enable verbose
.BR brk (2)
/
.BR sbrk (2)
VM growth diagnostics.
.TP
.B perso:linux
Enable Linux-personality-specific loader diagnostics.
.TP
.B perso:linux:aout
Enable Linux a.out loader tracing: ZMAGIC/QMAGIC/OMAGIC layout decisions and
.BR uselib (2)
DLL mapping.
.TP
.B perso:elks
Enable ELKS personality diagnostics broadly.
This parent channel also enables more specific ELKS channels.
.TP
.B perso:elks:aout
Enable ELKS a.out loader tracing.
.TP
.B perso:elks:syscall
Enable ELKS unsupported-syscall diagnostics.
.TP
.B perso:elks:trap
Enable ELKS Minix trap diagnostics.
.TP
.B perso:elks:kmem
Enable ELKS synthetic
.B /dev/kmem
snapshot tracing, including exported task-slot order and kmem seek/read offsets.
.TP
.B perso:native
Enable native-personality ELF loader/exec diagnostics.
.TP
.B perso:freebsd
Enable FreeBSD-personality loader diagnostics.
.TP
.B perso:xenix:xout
Enable Xenix/386 x.out loader tracing: segment layout and the split-fragment
size decisions the loader has to make from a truncated binary.
.TP
.B perso:xenix:syscall
Enable Xenix syscall tracing through the
.B lcall $7
trap emulation path.
.TP
.B perso_lookup
Log personality lookups made on the syscall dispatch path, naming the
personality selected for the calling process.
Note the underscore: this channel predates the colon convention and is
.I not
a child of
.BR perso ,
so
.B debug=perso
does not enable it.
Ask for it by name.
.TP
.B syscall_stats
Enable per-syscall statistics accounting (counts/timing) in addition to the
.B syscall
entry/return trace.
.TP
.B ext2trace
Enable ext2 directory-operation tracing (link/unlink/rename and dirent
add/remove), limited to low inode numbers to keep the channel readable.
.TP
.B vm_leak
Enable VM page/pager leak accounting: report the running
.BR alloc \- dealloc
balance of vnode-pager objects at process exit so growth is visible
(healthy steady state is zero).
.TP
.B xfd
Enable extended file-descriptor lifecycle tracing.
.TP
.B xsig
Enable extended signal delivery/return tracing.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
The following older boot flags remain supported:
.TP
.B syscall_trace
Equivalent to enabling the
.B syscall
channel.
.TP
.B syscall_log
Equivalent to enabling the
.B syscall
channel.
.PP
.B serial_debug
is separate from
.BR debug= .
It selects serial console mirroring; it does not by itself enable any debug
channel.
.PP
Four names are additionally accepted as bare flags, so
.B trap
means the same as
.BR debug=trap :
.BR trap ,
.BR xsig ,
.B xfd
and
.BR nosigalrm .
Prefer the
.B debug=
form for the first three; only
.B nosigalrm
is primarily a bare flag, since it changes behaviour rather than logging.
.SH RELATED PARAMETERS
.TP
.B console=console0
Use the default screen console.
.TP
.BI console=serial N
Route the console through COM\c
.IR N+1 .
.TP
.B video=text
Keep the system on the hardware text console and suppress framebuffer-console takeover.
.TP
.BI video=text: COLS x ROWS
Request a specific hardware text-console geometry.
This is equivalent to
.BR textmode= COLS x ROWS .
.TP
.BI textmode= COLS x ROWS
Select the hardware text-console geometry used by the kernel VT driver.
The bottom physical row is reserved for the kernel status line, so the visible tty geometry reported to userland is one row smaller than the selected physical mode.
On BIOS
.B zImage
and floppy boots, the setup stub applies supported text modes in real mode before entering the kernel proper.
.TP
.B vga=ask
On BIOS
.B zImage
and floppy boots, invoke the real-mode VGA text-mode selector before protected-mode handoff.
The current selector offers
.BR 80x25 ,
.BR 80x43 ,
and
.BR 80x50 .
When the BIOS advertises VBE text modes, the selector also adds any detected
.BR 132x25 ,
.BR 132x43 ,
.BR 132x50 ,
and
.BR 132x60
entries.
It then appends the matching
.B textmode=
token so the higher-half kernel uses the same geometry.
.PP
Currently supported kernel text-console geometries are:
.IP \(bu 2
.B 80x25
(visible tty area:
.BR 80x24 )
.IP \(bu
.B 80x43
(visible tty area:
.BR 80x42 )
.IP \(bu
.B 80x50
(visible tty area:
.BR 80x49 )
.PP
BIOS-selected text modes such as
.BR 80x43 ,
.BR 132x25 ,
.BR 132x43 ,
.BR 132x50 ,
and
.B 132x60
are honored after the real-mode setup path has programmed them.
The higher-half kernel does not fake those modes on its own; it only reprograms the stable built-in
.BR 80x25
and
.B 80x50
cases directly.
.TP
.BI root= device
Select the root block device.
.TP
.BI rootfstype= type [ ,type ... ]
Select one or more root filesystem probe targets.
Each is tried in the order given and the first that mounts wins.
The value
.B auto
probes the built-in list
.RB ( ext2 ,
.BR fat ,
.BR minix ,
.BR udf ,
in that order) and may appear on its own or among named types.
Omitting
.B rootfstype=
entirely is the same as
.BR auto .
.TP
.B ro
Mount the root filesystem read-only.
The kernel's own default is read-write, so a direct kernel boot that says
nothing comes up writable; every GRUB menu entry passes
.B ro
explicitly, because a bootloader boot is the one that can run
.BR e2fsck (8)
before anything has written to the filesystem.
Bring it up read-write once the check passes:
.PP
.RS
.EX
mount \-o remount,rw /
.EE
.RE
.PP
.RS
ext2 honours this at mount time \(em writes are refused with
.B EROFS
\(em and refuses a read-write remount outright if the volume was flagged
unsafe, so a filesystem needing repair cannot be quietly written to.
.RE
.TP
.B rw
Mount the root filesystem read-write.
This is the default.
.B ro
and
.B rw
are resolved last-one-wins, which is what makes the option useful at the
GRUB menu: press
.B e
on an entry, append
.B rw
to the line, and it overrides the
.B ro
the entry already carries without your having to find and delete it.
.TP
.BI init= path
Select the initial userspace program.
.TP
.BI initarg= argument
Pass a single argument to the program selected by
.BR init= .
Ignored unless
.B init=
is also given.
.SH USB CONTROLLER OPTIONS
These exist because the paths they control touch state that only real firmware
sets up, and cannot be exercised under emulation.
A machine that one of them wedges has to stay bootable.
.TP
.B nousbhandoff
Skip the USB Legacy Support BIOS-to-OS handoff on both xHCI and EHCI.
Normally the kernel claims each controller from SMM before resetting it, since
firmware that emulates a PS/2 keyboard from a USB one keeps driving the same
registers otherwise.
Use this if claiming the controller is itself what hangs the boot.
.TP
.B xhciroute
Perform the Intel port reroute, which is otherwise
.I not
done.
On Intel PCHs the shared USB2 ports come out of reset wired to the companion
EHCI; this moves them to the xHCI.
It is opt-in because the
.B XUSB2PR
write wedges some machines outright \(em on a Lenovo C460 under a legacy BIOS
boot the kernel stops dead on it, while the identical write survives under
UEFI, which implicates the firmware's legacy USB emulation handling it in SMM.
Leaving the ports with the EHCI costs nothing: the reroute decides only which
controller owns a USB2 port, not whether the port works.
Enable it if you want USB2 devices driven by the xHCI and your firmware
tolerates the switch.
.TP
.B noehci
Do not attach the EHCI driver at all.
Since the reroute is opt-in, the USB2 ports normally stay with the companion
EHCI, so this makes every device on them \(em including a root filesystem on a
USB stick \(em invisible.
Combining it with a boot that does not also pass
.B xhciroute
is how you get a kernel that panics for want of a root device.
It costs nothing only when
.B xhciroute
has moved every USB2 port to the xHCI.
.TP
.B xhcidebug
Trace xHCI bring-up: controller reset, ring allocation, run, and port power.
A boot that stops during xHCI initialisation then names the register access
that did it rather than going silent.
.TP
.B ehcidebug
The same for EHCI bring-up: reset, DMA structure allocation, run, port power.
.SH DIAGNOSTIC AND WORKAROUND FLAGS
.TP
.B nosmp
.TQ
.B up
Boot uniprocessor: stop after the bootstrap processor and start no
application processors.
Discovery is skipped entirely rather than the extra CPUs being started and
parked, so nothing runs on them at any point.
This is the cheapest way to establish whether a fault, a lock-up or a
corruption needs more than one CPU to reproduce \(em on an emulator you could
pass a CPU count instead, but on real hardware there is no other way to ask.
.TP
.BI trace_pid= pid
Restrict syscall tracing to a single process.
Has no effect unless syscall tracing is enabled through
.B debug=syscall
or one of its compatibility flags.
.TP
.B syscall_trace_serial
Route the syscall trace stream straight to the UART instead of the kernel
console, so userland output on the screen stays legible while the trace is
captured separately.
.TP
.B procmem
Dump the address space of PID 1 before panicking when init dies.
.TP
.B nosigalrm
Force
.B SIGALRM
and
.B SIGIO
to be ignored process-wide.
A workaround for foreign-personality binaries that install a handler and are
then killed by the first timer or input event.
.TP
.B mousedbg
Log every USB HID mouse report (deltas, wheel, button transitions).
.TP
.B audio_null
Register the null audio backend, so the audio stack can be exercised on a
machine with no sound hardware.
.TP
.B bga=nolfb
Force the Bochs/QEMU BGA display driver into banked mode instead of using its
linear framebuffer.
.TP
.BI textmode_bios= 1
Trust the text-console geometry the BIOS setup stub already programmed rather
than reprogramming the CRTC in the higher-half kernel.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR execve (2),
.BR signal (2),
.BR rootfs (7),
.BR personality_elks (7)
